From: Justin Casey <4348807+justincasey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 19:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] V1 engine: the prefix KV cache persists across restarts

With DSV41_KV_PERSIST=1 the engine core can save its prefix cache before a
stop and restores it at the next start, so a prompt cached before a restart
is a prefix hit after it.

A prefix hit is a scheduler decision over the BlockPool's hash table, and
every hit block lives at the same block id in each rank's single KV backing
allocation (all cache groups are views into it). The snapshot is therefore
each rank's whole backing buffer plus the scheduler's table (per-block hash
keys and token counts, the hash map in insertion order, the free queue in
eviction order); restoring both at identical block ids reproduces the
in-process cache, and request-private state (compressor rings, the CED
decoder and draft sliding windows) is rebuilt by the usual replay after a
hit, as in-process.

Save: EngineCore.save_kv_snapshot(abort), reachable as POST
/v1/dsv41/kv_snapshot[?abort=true] (under /v1, so --api-key guards it;
registered only with DSV41_KV_PERSIST=1). It refuses while requests are
running unless abort, which pauses the scheduler (aborting them, the cache
kept), saves once no step is in flight, and resumes. Every block must be
free, so each hashed block's contents are final. The old table is deleted
first; every rank writes its buffer through weight_snapshot's staged
O_DIRECT pipeline with a CRC32 per 64 MiB chunk into a temporary directory
renamed into place, tagged with a fresh save epoch; the table, with the same
epoch, is written last and only if every rank succeeded. An empty cache
saves nothing and deletes the old snapshot.

Restore: at the end of EngineCore.__init__, after warmup (which writes real
block ids) and once the scheduler exists. The table is checked first (key,
COMPLETE marker, a permutation free queue, block ids in range, hash map and
block keys in agreement); then every rank checks its own key and the epoch
and reads its buffer, and the table is installed only if every rank
succeeded. Otherwise the cache starts empty (bytes some ranks overwrote are
unreachable). A snapshot that fails an integrity check (marker, size,
CRC32, table consistency) is deleted, and with it the table of an
incomplete set. Logs: "KV snapshot saved/restored N GiB per rank x R ranks,
M cached blocks (E hash entries) in X s".

The key covers the checkpoint (as weight_snapshot keys it), the vLLM, B12X
and TileLang sources, Torch, CUDA, the Python version, a probe of the block
hash function (sha256 hashes pickle their input), vLLM's config hash, the
resolved HF config, the cache configuration, the scheduler's KV cache
configuration (groups, specs, layers, tensor placement, number of blocks,
layout), the block, scheduler-block and hash-block sizes, the hash algorithm,
the NONE_HASH seed and value (digests only), the parallel layout, the engine
environment (without the names vLLM sets afresh in every process) and
DSV41_KV_PERSIST_TAG. Each rank adds its own checkpoint copy, sources, device
and KV cache configuration. Unsupported configurations keep
the feature off with a logged reason: data parallel, a KV connector, KV
cache events, boundary checkpoints, HiSparse host groups, a random per-process
NONE_HASH seed.

weight_snapshot's key leaves out the DSV41_KV_PERSIST* switches, so turning
this on does not invalidate weight snapshots, and both keys leave out
VLLM_OBJECT_STORAGE_SHM_BUFFER_NAME (envs.py sets a fresh uuid4 in every
process tree) and VLLM_DIST_IDENT (the CPU worker's rendezvous port or file),
which otherwise change the key at every boot.

Snapshots live under DSV41_KV_PERSIST_DIR (default
/cache/dsv41/kv-snapshots), owner-only, DSV41_KV_PERSIST_KEEP per rank
(default 1); DSV41_KV_PERSIST_VERIFY=0 skips the CRC32 check on restore.

Tests: tests/v1/core/test_kv_snapshot.py (CPU).

